if: Run Code Conditionally
sbp &lt;- 165

if (sbp &gt;= 180) {
  print("Hypertensive crisis: seek care immediately")
} else if (sbp &gt;= 140) {
  print("Stage 2 hypertension")
} else if (sbp &gt;= 130) {
  print("Stage 1 hypertension")
} else {
  print("Not hypertensive")
}

The condition must be a single TRUE or FALSE.
Branches are checked top to bottom; the first match wins: order matters!

The for Loop
Repeat a code block once per element of a vector:
sites &lt;- c("Brooklyn", "Queens", "Bronx")

for (s in sites) {
  print(paste("Auditing site:", s))
}
# Looping over positions with seq_along()
wait_times &lt;- c(45, 12, 85, 30)

for (i in seq_along(wait_times)) {
  print(paste("Patient", i, "waited", wait_times[i], "minutes"))
}

Use seq_along(x), never 1:length(x): it behaves correctly when x is empty.

Combining Loops with Last Week’s Tools
Loop over groups, applying a full pipeline each time:
continents &lt;- c("Africa", "Asia", "Europe")

for (ct in continents) {
  n_hi &lt;- gm |&gt;
    filter(year == 2008, continent == ct,
           !is.na(life_expectancy), life_expectancy &gt;= 75) |&gt;
    nrow()
  print(paste(ct, "has", n_hi, "countries with life expectancy &gt;= 75"))
}
Next week: purrr::map() will make this pattern even cleaner.

AI Corner: The Unnecessary Loop
Asked to flag high-mortality countries, an AI wrote:
flags &lt;- c()
for (i in 1:nrow(df)) {
  if (is.na(df$child_mortality[i])) {
    flags &lt;- c(flags, NA)
  } else if (df$child_mortality[i] &gt; 50) {
    flags &lt;- c(flags, "high")
  } else {
    flags &lt;- c(flags, "low")
  }
}
Audit findings:

Growing flags with c() re-copies the vector every iteration: quadratic slowdown.
1:nrow(df) breaks on an empty data frame (1:0 runs twice!).
Three branches of loop for what one vectorized line does:

flags &lt;- ifelse(df$child_mortality &gt; 50, "high", "low")   # NA stays NA for free






Important


Correct output is not enough: audit AI code for idiom and scalability too.

Grouping by Multiple Variables
gm |&gt;
  filter(year %in% c(1998, 2008), !is.na(life_expectancy)) |&gt;
  group_by(continent, year) |&gt;
  summarize(mean_le = mean(life_expectancy), .groups = "drop")

One row per continent × year combination.
.groups = "drop" returns an ordinary ungrouped tibble (and silences the message).







Warning


A grouped tibble stays grouped! Later verbs operate within groups until you ungroup() or use .groups = "drop".

pivot_wider(): Long → Wide
The inverse: spread a name-value pair back into columns (great for human-readable tables):
bp_long |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = visit, values_from = bp)
# Classic use: a summary table with one column per year
gm |&gt;
  filter(year %in% c(1998, 2008), !is.na(life_expectancy)) |&gt;
  group_by(continent, year) |&gt;
  summarize(mean_le = mean(life_expectancy), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = year, values_from = mean_le)

A Full Mini-Analysis, End to End
“Which continents improved life expectancy most between 1998 and 2008?”
gm |&gt;
  filter(year %in% c(1998, 2008)) |&gt;
  drop_na(life_expectancy) |&gt;
  group_by(continent, year) |&gt;
  summarize(mean_le = mean(life_expectancy), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = year, values_from = mean_le,
              names_prefix = "y") |&gt;
  mutate(gain = y2008 - y1998) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(gain))
Six verbs, one readable story: filter → clean → group → summarize → reshape → derive.

str_detect() Powers filter()
# gm rows for countries whose name contains "stan"
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008, str_detect(country, "stan")) |&gt;
  select(country, life_expectancy)
# Case-insensitive matching: normalize first
gm |&gt;
  filter(str_detect(str_to_lower(country), "united"))
Pattern matching + last week’s verbs = powerful cohort selection.

fct_reorder(): Order Levels by a Statistic
The #1 factor trick for readable plots: order categories by their value, not alphabetically.
library(ggplot2)

gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  drop_na(life_expectancy, region) |&gt;
  group_by(region) |&gt;
  summarize(mean_le = mean(life_expectancy)) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = mean_le, y = fct_reorder(region, mean_le))) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  labs(y = NULL, x = "Mean life expectancy (2008)")
Bars now rise in order: instantly readable.

fct_lump(): Collapse Rare Categories
Too many categories? Keep the most common, lump the rest into "Other":
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  drop_na(region) |&gt;
  mutate(region_top = fct_lump(region, n = 5)) |&gt;
  count(region_top, sort = TRUE)

fct_lump(f, n = 5): keep the 5 largest levels + "Other".
Also useful: fct_infreq() (order by frequency), fct_rev() (reverse).

arrange(): Ordering Rows
# Lowest child mortality first
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  arrange(child_mortality)

# Highest first: wrap in desc()
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(life_expectancy))

# Tie-breakers: sort by continent, then by population within continent
gm |&gt; arrange(continent, desc(population))

NA values are always placed last, regardless of direction.

mutate(): New Columns from Old
mutate() adds columns to the end of the data frame; existing columns are preserved.
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  select(country, population, GDP_per_capita) |&gt;
  mutate(gdp_total = GDP_per_capita * population)

Right side is any vectorized expression (Week 2 arithmetic!).
New columns can be built from columns you just created:

gm |&gt;
  mutate(gdp_total    = GDP_per_capita * population,
         gdp_billions = gdp_total / 1e9)

mutate() with Logical Columns
A comparison creates a TRUE/FALSE column, ready for later filtering:
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  select(country, life_expectancy) |&gt;
  mutate(above_80 = life_expectancy &gt; 80)
# Unit conversions are classic mutate() one-liners
gm |&gt;
  mutate(pop_millions = population / 1e6)






Warning


mutate() never changes the original gm object. To keep your work, assign the result: gm2008_clean &lt;- gm |&gt; ...

Putting It All Together
A complete wrangling pipeline, start to finish:
smoking_2008 &lt;- gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008, !is.na(smoking_male), !is.na(smoking_female)) |&gt;
  select(country, continent, population,
         starts_with("smoking")) |&gt;
  rename(male = smoking_male, female = smoking_female) |&gt;
  mutate(gap = male - female,
         pop_millions = population / 1e6) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(gap))

head(smoking_2008)
Every verb answers one question. The pipeline tells the whole story.

Debugging Pipelines: Read Them Like Steps
Run a pipeline one verb at a time to find where things go wrong:
gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008)            # Step 1: run up to here. Rows look right?

gm |&gt;
  filter(year == 2008) |&gt;
  select(country, sugar)          # Step 2: expected columns present?

Highlight everything up to (but not including) a pipe and press Cmd/Ctrl + Enter.
nrow(), names(), and head() are your cheap diagnostic probes.

